Policy "Parameterized SQL only":
SQL must be built with parameters or an ORM, never by concatenating or formatting strings with values that come from outside the program. Table and column names taken from constants in the code are fine.

<untrusted_code>
File: src/UserRepository.cs
Lines: 15-15
Rule message: Possible violation of the policy "Parameterized SQL only".

Code (flagged lines marked >>):
       1 | using System.Data.SqlClient;
       2 | 
       3 | public class UserRepository
       4 | {
       5 |     private const string TableName = "Users";
       6 |     private readonly SqlConnection _connection;
       7 | 
       8 |     public UserRepository(SqlConnection connection)
       9 |     {
      10 |         _connection = connection;
      11 |     }
      12 | 
      13 |     public void Delete(string userId)
      14 |     {
>>    15 |         var cmd = new SqlCommand("DELETE FROM Users WHERE UserId = '" + userId + "'", _connection);
      16 |         cmd.ExecuteNonQuery();
      17 |     }
      18 | 
      19 |     public SqlDataReader Find(int id)
      20 |     {
      21 |         var cmd = new SqlCommand($"SELECT * FROM {TableName} WHERE Id = @id", _connection);
      22 |         cmd.Parameters.AddWithValue("@id", id);
      23 |         return cmd.ExecuteReader();
      24 |     }
      25 | }
</untrusted_code>

Why the reviewer kept it: Line 15 builds the DELETE statement by concatenating the method argument userId directly into the SQL text instead of using a SqlParameter, so a crafted userId can inject SQL. This is production repository code, and the value is not a constant.

Answer with the JSON object only.
